---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import katex from 'katex';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const typeset = (latex: string, displayMode = false) =>
  katex.renderToString(latex, {
    displayMode,
    output: 'htmlAndMathml',
    throwOnError: true,
  });

const alpha = typeset(String.raw`\alpha`);
const beta = typeset(String.raw`\beta`);
const theta = typeset(String.raw`\theta`);
const thetaRange = typeset(String.raw`\theta_0,\ldots,\theta_k`);
const thetaSum = typeset(String.raw`\sum_{i=0}^{k}\theta_i`);
const thetaI = typeset(String.raw`\theta_i`);
const alphaCorrelation = typeset(String.raw`\operatorname{corr}(\alpha,\theta_i) \ne 0`);
const betaCorrelation = typeset(String.raw`\operatorname{corr}(\beta,\theta_i) \ne 0`);
const lambdaNonzero = typeset(String.raw`\lambda \ne 0`);
const utilityEquation = typeset(
  String.raw`\alpha = \lambda\beta + \sum_{i=0}^{k}\gamma_i\theta_i + \varepsilon`,
  true,
);
---

<Base
  title="On Love | Elisa Diop-Weyer"
  description="On Love, by emdw."
>
  <article class="essay-page">
    <a class="back-link" href={`${base}/musings/`}>← Selected Outputs</a>

    <header>
      <h1>On Love</h1>
      <p class="dedication">Pour Aurélien</p>
    </header>

    <div class="essay-body">
      <p>If person A loves person B:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>a decomposition of A’s utility function includes B’s utility function;</li>
        <li>the relationship between A and B’s utility function is not only mediated by a third factor <span class="math" set:html={theta}></span>.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>I think of (2) as something analogous to the exclusion restriction in econometrics;</p>

      <p>Consider A’s subjective utility <span class="math" set:html={alpha}></span>, B’s subjective utility <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span>, and a set of variables <span class="math" set:html={thetaRange}></span> that are correlated to both <span class="math" set:html={alpha}></span> and <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span>. If <span class="math" set:html={alpha}></span> and <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span> live together, then B’s good mood may be a third factor <span class="math" set:html={theta}></span> and A plausibly does nice things for B not because <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span> is an end in itself but because B’s good mood is correlated with their own utility. Other plausible <span class="math" set:html={theta}></span>s: the social rewards A garners for being nice to B, the internal normative validation A enjoys from acting with kindness and affection. In evolutionary terms, with regard to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kin_selection" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hamilton’s rule</a>, the inclusive fitness benefits that A enjoys by helping B are constitute a <span class="math" set:html={theta}></span> -- although I’d like to afford more thought to this. What’s Im saying here is that <span class="math" set:html={alpha}></span>’s correlation with <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span> must not be explicable only in terms of a combination of dual-correlates <span class="math" set:html={thetaSum}></span>.</p>

      <p>Of course, a set of <span class="math" set:html={theta}></span>s is to be expected in the relationship between <span class="math" set:html={alpha}></span> and <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span>, but the question is: are <span class="math" set:html={alpha}></span> and <span class="math" set:html={beta}></span> still causally associated in the absence of any <span class="math" set:html={theta}></span>. That is it say, if we have:</p>

      <div class="display-equation" set:html={utilityEquation}></div>

      <p>Where for each <span class="math" set:html={thetaI}></span>, <span class="math" set:html={alphaCorrelation}></span> and <span class="math" set:html={betaCorrelation}></span>,</p>

      <p>then A loves B if and only if <span class="math" set:html={lambdaNonzero}></span>.</p>

      <p>I think this definition remains a phenomenological one by nature of the fact that we’re talking about utility, which in my use here is experienced and subjective.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="signature">-- emdw 20261007</p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .essay-page {
    width: min(100%, 48rem);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.8em;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .back-link:hover,
  .back-link:focus-visible {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  h1 {
    font-style: italic;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .dedication {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .essay-body {
    margin-top: 3rem;
  }

  .essay-body p {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
  }

  ol {
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 2.25rem;
    list-style-type: decimal;
  }

  ol li {
    margin-bottom: 0.65rem;
    padding-left: 0.2rem;
  }

  ol li::marker {
    content: counter(list-item) ")  ";
  }

  .math {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .math :global(.katex) {
    font-size: 1em;
  }

  .display-equation {
    margin: 2rem auto;
    overflow-x: auto;
    overflow-y: hidden;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .display-equation :global(.katex-display) {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .display-equation :global(.katex) {
    font-size: 1.25em;
  }

  .signature {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    text-align: right;
  }
</style>
